  "why_this_does_not_prove_objectionable_video_quality": [
    "Author categories label intentional frame manipulation, which may be aesthetically appropriate and not a human visual defect, unlike production stutter.",
    "No precise human-confirmed altered frame interval references, so temporal localization precision/F1 cannot be computed.",
    "Temporal positives and non-temporal edited controls are editorial categories; other edits and reencodings can cause motion/appearance changes and temporal control false alarms.",
    "All selected clips came from first release half and possibly correlated original Vimeo content; 38 source clips not 38 independent camera families.",
    "Reference-aware frame matching requires access to actual associated original source; where perceptual hashes disagree it must abstain instead of marking good.",
    "A simple duration ratio baseline may perform well for author speed changes without seeing pixels, and should be reported as a confound, not a quality analyzer.",
    "Benchmark size nineteen per class means even no observed false positives is too weak for near-perfect high-stakes specificity.",
    "A method comparison on this dataset is model selection; another untouched video source group is needed for unbiased deployment acceptance."
  ],
  "role_for_actual_production": "Use original source/video hashes, exact approved edit/hold timeline and native PTS first, then frame content comparison when exact final approved reference is available, use no-reference freeze/flow as candidate trigger only; ordinary optical flow and visual LLM confidence cannot certify physical plausibility, object realism or stutter intent."
